work-order.text:
{
  "acceptance": [
    {
      "check": "Signed-in member creates expenses with description, amount, date, category and note as single-payer and multi-payer for all four split types",
      "id": "AC-1"
    },
    {
      "check": "Shortfall errors name the shortfall and size, remainder lands on first payer, reopening shows stored split type and inputs exactly as entered",
      "id": "AC-2"
    },
    {
      "check": "Newest-first list with member/category filter plus search; edit/delete work, failed saves keep input, delete confirm names the expense",
      "id": "AC-3"
    },
    {
      "check": "Unit tests prove integer math, sum preservation, remainder placement across generated splits, plus failing writes leaving no rows",
      "id": "AC-4"
    },
    {
      "check": "Signed-out and stranger callers get indistinguishable unauthenticated/not-found answers on expense reads and writes",
      "id": "AC-5"
    },
    {
      "check": "Each create/edit/delete commits expense, payers, split lines and activity entry in one interactive transaction",
      "id": "AC-6"
    },
    {
      "check": "Money crosses boundaries only as integer minor units; percentages parsed to basis points at the zod boundary",
      "id": "AC-7"
    },
    {
      "check": "Expense editor and list follow token set with labelled controls, five states, group-currency amounts, usable on phone viewport",
      "id": "AC-8"
    },
    {
      "check": "A refused expense save shows each failed rule exactly once in the page: one visible shortfall sentence, not repeated across field error, live note and submit-row message",
      "id": "AC-9"
    },
    {
      "check": "Saving an expense edit without changing anything reports that nothing changed instead of Expense updated",
      "id": "AC-10"
    },
    {
      "check": "Expenses sharing a date list newest-first on every run: alpha, beta, gamma created in that order appear gamma, beta, alpha, including when the date was left as the default today",
      "id": "AC-11"
    }
  ],
  "approach": "Targeted fix on the PR branch, no rebuild. In lib/expenses/queries.ts change listExpenses to .orderBy(desc(expenses.date), desc(expenses.createdAt), desc(expenses.id)) and rewrite the block comment to say the tiebreak is creation time with id as the final deterministic fallback. In lib/db/schema.ts update only the expenses-table comment so it no longer claims the (group_id, date, id) index is exactly the list read; the index definition itself is untouched, so no migration is needed and the approved key text stays true. Add a unit case creating alpha, beta, gamma on one date and asserting listExpenses returns gamma, beta, alpha; QA re-runs the T-9 spec against the new criterion.",
  "files": [
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Change listExpenses ordering to desc(date), desc(createdAt), desc(id) and rewrite the ordering comment to name creation time as the same-day tiebreak",
      "path": "lib/expenses/queries.ts"
    },
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "change": "Update only the expenses-table comment to stop claiming the (group_id, date, id) index is exactly the list read; index definition and migration untouched",
      "path": "lib/db/schema.ts"
    }
  ],
  "issue": 6,
  "kind": "bug",
  "mode": "committed",
  "reproduced": false,
  "root_cause": "BUG-3: listExpenses orders same-date expenses by desc(expenses.id), but expenses.id is gen_random_uuid v4, so the tiebreak is arbitrary rather than creation order. expenses.createdAt exists but is not used in the ordering. Every expense created without touching the date shares today, so the tie case is the common case, and T-9 passed in attempt 1 only by luck of the draw.",
  "tests": [
    {
      "action": "edit",
      "cases": [
        "Three expenses created alpha, beta, gamma on the same date list as gamma, beta, alpha via listExpenses (T-9 repro at unit level)",
        "Expenses on different dates still list newest day first regardless of creation order"
      ],
      "path": "lib/expenses/actions.test.ts"
    }
  ],
  "understanding": "Attempt 2 (PR #20, plan v2) was right about what it targeted: the retest confirms BUG-1 (triple-rendered refusal) and BUG-2 (unchanged-save notice) are both fixed, and every AC-1/AC-2 plus AC-4..AC-10 rollup passes. What the plan got wrong stretches back to v1 and neither attempt noticed: the newest-first list orders same-date rows by desc(id), which the code comment calls a stable tiebreak - but expenses.id is gen_random_uuid v4, so the tiebreak is random, not creation order. T-9 passed in attempt 1 by luck (one draw in three matched) and failed in attempt 2, with three identical runs giving three different orders. Worse, the tie is the common case, not the edge: the editor defaults the date to today, so every expense created without touching the date shares it. The fix is not another patch around the v2 diagnosis, which stands; it is a first-time correction of the ordering the v1 plan assumed was deterministic.",
  "version": 3
}
